  "body": "## What this changes\n\nFlash Next's PLE n-gram gather (`Ngram.rows` in `families/flashnext/cuda_ple.zig`) spawned `min(128, items / 4)` threads per call and joined them. The gather itself is a few memcpys from the mapped table, so most of a call was thread start-up: about 65-80 \u00b5s per thread, 150 \u00b5s for one decode token, 1 ms for a 4-token batch (4 streams, or a drafted verify) and 10-11 ms per prompt chunk.\n\nThe table now keeps a pool for its life (`cuda_ple_pool.zig`, 139 lines with a unit test):\n- workers are spawned on first need, up to 127, and park on a futex between calls; `deinit` wakes and joins them;\n- a call's parts are the same `[lo, hi)` ranges as before, claimed from one counter by the caller and the workers. Each claim that leaves parts unclaimed wakes one more worker, so quick parts stay mostly on the caller and slow (faulting) parts fan out;\n- calls are serialised by a mutex, and the first failed part's error is returned, in part order, as before.\n\nOnly which thread copies a range changes, so the output bytes are identical by construction. A table without a pool (for example a path that builds `Ngram` some other way) gathers on the caller's thread.\n\nBranch `fn-ple-pool` @ `e4062e6a`, on `main` @ `bd264368a`.\n\n## Receipt\n\n- **Environment.** One GB10 (sm_121), CUDA 13.0, Zig 0.17.0. `nvidia/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-FP4-Local` @ `b9ce18a6` (its `tensors/` index loads since #1179).\n- **`rows()` on the real table**, p50, alternating old / new / old / new, output hashes identical at every size:\n\n| items | main | this PR |\n|---|---|---|\n| 16 (one decode token) | 165 / 163 \u00b5s | 18 / 22 \u00b5s |\n| 64 (4 tokens) | 1,020 / 971 \u00b5s | 31 / 32 \u00b5s |\n| 4,096 | 11.4 / 10.4 ms | 0.95 / 0.97 ms |\n| 8,192 | 11.5 / 10.7 ms | 1.66 / 1.66 ms |\n\n- **Served**, a fresh server per arm, main / this PR / main / this PR with `--no-drafts`, then one MTP pair:\n\n| | main | this PR | change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| c=1 ms/step | 42.00 / 42.14 | 41.90 / 41.74 | -0.1 / -0.4 (noise-sized) |\n| c=4 ms/step | 61.11 / 61.87 | 60.09 / 60.84 | -1.02 / -1.03 |\n| TTFT 8k | 6.365 / 6.481 s | 6.335 / 6.432 s | -0.03 / -0.05 s |\n| MTP c=1 ms/round (one run) | 60.52 | 59.44 | -1.08 |\n| MTP c=4 ms/round (one run) | 223.6 | 218.9 | -4.7 (-2.1 %) |\n\n  The c=4 gain has the same size and sign in both rounds. The microbench accounts for about 0.65 ms of it (4 lanes x 4 calls a round); the remaining ~0.35 ms is not explained. Prompt chunks mostly hide the old cost, because the batch path gathers on a side thread while the layers before the PLE layer run.\n- **Exactness.** Token hashes for greedy c=1 and c=4, seeded c=1 and c=4, and both turns of a resumed prompt-cache run: identical across all six servers, the MTP ones included.\n- **Tests.** Full `zig build test -Dnvcc=...` exit 0. lean_check 0 new. `zig fmt` clean.\n- **Contract.** No kernel change.\n\n## Not run, and why\n\n- Metal: `cuda_ple.zig` belongs to the CUDA family only, so there was nothing to run there.\n- The one-thread gather `cuda_batch.zig` starts per pass (~28 \u00b5s) could use the same pool; left for a separate change.\n\n## Overlap\n\n#1113 sets the thread count for its EXL3 table in the same function and builds that table without going through `Ngram.init`, so a rebase there needs a hand-merge; its table then simply gathers on the caller's thread, as before.\n",
